## Notes on the design decisions

- **`:hover:not(:disabled)`** — the `:not(:disabled)` guard is important; otherwise a disabled button still lights up on hover, which feels broken.
- **`:focus-visible` instead of `:focus`** — gives keyboard users a visible ring without flashing a ring every time someone clicks with a mouse.
- **Extending `ButtonHTMLAttributes`** — the component forwards `onClick`, `aria-*`, `form`, `name`, `value`, etc. straight to the underlying `<button>`, so it behaves like a real button in every context (forms, toolbars, etc.).
- **`type="button"` default** — prevents accidental form submissions when the button lives inside a `<form>`.
- **Disabled styling** uses a lighter blue plus `cursor: not-allowed` rather than just `opacity`, so it stays legible on colored backgrounds. If you prefer a uniform look across many button variants, swap it for `opacity: 0.5;` instead.

Here's a ready-to-use React product card component with image, title, price, and optional rating and cart button.
```jsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import './ProductCard.css';

export default function ProductCard({
  image,
  title,
  price,
  currency = 'USD',
  rating,
  onAddToCart,
}) {
  const [imgFailed, setImgFailed] = useState(false);

  const formattedPrice = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', {
    style: 'currency',
    currency,
  }).format(price);

  const roundedRating = typeof rating === 'number' ? Math.round(rating) : null;

  return (
    <article className="product-card">
      <div className="product-card__media">
        {imgFailed ? (
          <div className="product-card__placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
            No image
          </div>
        ) : (
          <img
            className="product-card__image"
            src={image}
            alt={title}
            loading="lazy"
            onError={() => setImgFailed(true)}
          />
        )}
      </div>

      <div className="product-card__body">
        <h3 className="product-card__title">{title}</h3>

        {roundedRating !== null && (
          <div
            className="product-card__rating"
            aria-label={`Rated ${rating} out of 5`}
          >
            <span aria-hidden="true">
              {'★'.repeat(roundedRating)}
              {'☆'.repeat(5 - roundedRating)}
            </span>
          </div>
        )}

        <p className="product-card__price">{formattedPrice}</p>

        {onAddToCart && (
          <button
            type="button"
            className="product-card__button"
            onClick={() => onAddToCart({ image, title, price })}
          >
            Add to cart
          </button>
        )}
      </div>
    </article>
  );
}
```
### Product Card Features & Interactions

This card packs a few thoughtful touches that make it feel polished and ready for real use.

- **Image fallback** – If the product image fails to load, a “No image” placeholder appears instead of a broken icon.
- **Price formatting** – The price is automatically formatted with `Intl.NumberFormat`, using the `currency` prop (defaults to USD).
- **Optional rating** – When a numeric `rating` is passed, it renders a five-star display with filled and empty stars.
- **Add to cart callback** – If `onAddToCart` is provided, a button appears and calls the handler with the product data.
- **Hover and focus states** – The card lifts on hover, the image zooms slightly, and the button has a visible focus ring for keyboard users.
---

**Optimization Tip:** You can replace the hardcoded `'en-US'` locale in `Intl.NumberFormat` with a prop if you need to support multiple regions.

